.TH LCOVMERGE 1 "2026-10-10" "lcovmerge 1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lcovmerge \- LCOV .info file merger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lcovmerge
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIa.info\fR \fIb.info\fR ... \fB-o\fR \fIout.info\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lcovmerge
merges already-exported LCOV .info files. It does not collect raw coverage
or generate reports. Output records are written in a stable order, and
summary rows are recalculated from merged coverage records.
.PP
The --mem-limit setting limits memory reserved for coverage records while
sorting. It is not a cap on total process memory or temporary disk use.
--tmpdir selects the directory for temporary sorted files.
.PP
The output option is required. List-file paths are read relative to the
current working directory; empty lines and lines beginning with # are
ignored. Nested list files are limited to eight levels. Prefix an argument
with @@ to name a literal input path beginning with @.
.PP
The shell expands wildcard arguments before execution. Use \fB@listfile\fR to
read additional input paths, one per line. A single \fB-\fR input reads
standard input; output \fB-\fR writes standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -o " FILE" ", " --output " FILE"
Write the result to FILE. The destination is replaced only after a successful
file merge. Use \fB-\fR for standard output. Bytes already written to stdout
cannot be rolled back; a later signal or write failure may leave partial
output in the stream.
.TP
.BI --mem-limit " SIZE"
Set the limit for memory reserved for coverage records while sorting.
SIZE is bytes or an integer with optional K, M, or G binary suffix (multiples
of 1024; suffix matching is case-insensitive). The default is 64M; the
minimum is 8M per job. This setting does not cap total process memory or
temporary disk use; parser buffers, merge bookkeeping, thread stacks, and
runtime state need additional memory.
.TP
.BI --tmpdir " DIR"
Place temporary sorted files under an existing, writable DIR with enough
free disk space. The default is the system temporary directory. Files are
removed on success and handled errors. On POSIX they are also removed after
caught SIGINT, SIGTERM, and SIGHUP. A forced kill or machine failure can leave
files behind. Windows does not promise the same interruption cleanup.
.TP
.BI -j " N" ", " --jobs " N"
Process whole input files concurrently with N workers. N must be from 1
through 32. A single large input file is not split across workers. The
default is the lesser of four and the detected processor count, further
reduced when needed to fit the memory setting. An explicit count must fit
the minimum 8M per job. When all inputs are regular files and rewritten
records are already in stable order, the streaming merge uses one worker and
ignores this value. An out-of-order row, unsupported stream type, or
streaming-path memory limit causes a retry using temporary sorted files and
this worker count.
.TP
.BI --prefix-strip " PREFIX"
Remove a matching leading prefix from each SF path. A match must end at a
path boundary; following separators are removed.
.TP
.BI --rebase " OLD=NEW"
Replace a matching SF path prefix. May be repeated. Directory separators are
preserved from the input where possible.
.TP
.BI --include " GLOB"
Keep SF paths matching GLOB. May be repeated; when present, a path must
match at least one include.
.TP
.BI --exclude " GLOB"
Drop SF paths matching GLOB. Exclusions take precedence over includes.
Matching supports \fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, and bracket ranges.
.PP
Path rewriting runs in this order: rebasing, prefix stripping, then include
and exclude filtering. Rewritten paths that become identical are merged.
.TP
.BI --branch-coverage " on|off"
Keep BRDA records (default on) or omit them.
.TP
.B --no-function-data
Omit FN, FNDA, FNL, and FNA records.
.TP
.B --strict-checksum
Return input/format error status 2 when non-empty checksums for the same
source line disagree. The default warns and selects the lexicographically
smallest non-empty checksum.
.TP
.B --warn-unknown
Print a diagnostic for every preserved unknown record.
.TP
.B -q
Suppress verbose progress output.
.TP
.B -v
Print parsed byte/record totals, selected worker count, and merged summary
totals to standard error.
.TP
.B --stats
Print merged LF/LH/FNF/FNH/BRF/BRH totals to standard error.
.TP
.B --version
Print the version and build's git commit identifier.
.TP
.BI -h ", " --help
Print usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Merge LCOV files from test shards:
.PP
.nf
lcovmerge coverage/shard-*.info -o coverage/merged.info
.fi
.PP
Create a temporary directory and read paths from a list file:
.PP
.nf
tmpdir=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/lcovmerge
mkdir -p "$tmpdir"
lcovmerge --mem-limit 128M --tmpdir "$tmpdir" \\
  @coverage-inputs.txt -o merged.info
.fi
.PP
Rebase paths from two workers, then filter source files:
.PP
.nf
lcovmerge --rebase /build/worker-a/project=/workspace/project \\
  --rebase /build/worker-b/project=/workspace/project \\
  --include '*/src/*' --exclude '*/src/generated/*' \\
  shards/*.info -o merged.info
.fi
.SH INPUT RECORDS
Recognized rows include TN, SF/KF, legacy FN/FNDA, LCOV 2.x FNL/FNA, DA
with optional checksum, BRDA, MCDC, and summary rows. Summary rows in inputs
are ignored and generated from the merged coverpoints. Unknown colon-delimited
rows inside a source section are preserved in sorted order but are not
interpreted as coverage data; exact duplicates are preserved too.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Merge completed.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid or incomplete command-line options.
.TP
.B 2
Input or tracefile format error, including strict checksum disagreement.
.TP
.B 3
Input/output or temporary-file I/O failure, allocation failure, or
interruption.
.SH INTERRUPTION AND CLEANUP
On POSIX, SIGINT, SIGTERM, and SIGHUP request cooperative interruption.
A caught interruption returns status 3 and removes temporary sorted files
and any unpublished staged output. SIGKILL and machine failure cannot run
cleanup.
.PP
On POSIX, lcovmerge ignores SIGPIPE, so writing to a closed pipe reports
EPIPE as an I/O failure and follows normal cleanup. Bytes already written to
stdout remain visible if a later signal or write failure occurs.
.PP
Windows uses synchronous ReadFile and WriteFile calls and blocking worker
thread joins without a cancellation path. A blocked operation can prevent
interruption from completing; the interruption and cleanup guarantee is
POSIX-only.
.SH SEE ALSO
lcov(1), geninfo(1), genhtml(1)
